// Preload: what the language itself refers to, written in Rig. Every
// program imports it without an #import (spec 2.14): an ordinary program,
// a build program and each of its workspaces, rig check, a program the test
// mode runs interpreted (unit-tests --interpret), and the world of #run
// while compiling. rig finds it where it finds every module that ships with
// it, and reads this file right after the program's main file. In load
// order it comes first, before the main file (spec 2.13), so its
// declarations come first: a program that declares a name Preload declares
// is told so at its own declaration. Writing
//
//     #import "Preload";
//
// is allowed and reads nothing more, just as a #load of this file by its
// path reads nothing more: a file is read once.
//
// It holds the allocator's shape and the heap, type information and Any,
// and the context, with the temporary storage and its allocator.
//
// What Preload declares joins the program's one top level, so a program
// can't declare those names. README.md lists the ones that are for
// programs. Every name Preload declares for its own use starts with two
// underscores (__malloc, say), which leaves the plain names to programs:
// a convention, which the compiler doesn't enforce.

// The allocator: where memory comes from. Whatever needs memory takes an
// allocator, so the caller decides where it comes from and how it goes
// back; left out, it is the context's, context.allocator (below). Preload
// holds its shape, its modes and the heap, which every program has; Basic
// adds the helpers (alloc, resize, free and free_all), an arena, a pool,
// and the temporary storage's own helpers, talloc, its marks and its reset.
//
// An allocator is Jai's shape: one procedure and the data it works on. The
// procedure is called with a mode, ALLOCATE, RESIZE, FREE or FREE_ALL, and
// does what the mode says, with the data as its last argument: an arena's
// procedure gets its Arena, a pool's its Pool, the heap's nothing. Anything
// that holds an Allocator can be given any of them, or one a program writes
// itself, without knowing which.

// The modes an allocator procedure is called with (constants until enums).
ALLOCATE :: 0;
RESIZE :: 1;
FREE :: 2;
FREE_ALL :: 3;

// Jai's shape: one procedure and its data. The procedure type is written
// out, since Rig has no type aliases yet.
//
// The procedure is called as proc(mode, size, old_size, old_memory, data):
//
// - ALLOCATE: size bytes, or null.
// - RESIZE: old_memory, which holds old_size bytes, made size bytes long,
//   moved or not, with the first of its old bytes kept; or null, and then
//   old_memory is as it was.
// - FREE: gives old_memory back. Returns null.
// - FREE_ALL: gives back everything the allocator gave. Returns null.
Allocator :: struct {
    proc: (mode: int, size: int, old_size: int, old_memory: *void, data: *void) -> *void;
    data: *void;
}

// C's heap, malloc, realloc and free, declared under Preload's own names
// (#foreign libc "NAME", spec 7.6), so that a program keeps C's: it may
// declare C's malloc, realloc or free itself, and its free and __free are
// the same C function.
__malloc :: (size: u64) -> *void #foreign libc "malloc";
__realloc :: (memory: *void, size: u64) -> *void #foreign libc "realloc";
__free :: (memory: *void) #foreign libc "free";

// The heap: C's malloc, realloc and free, through Preload's own
// declarations. Its memory is aligned for any type, as C's is.
//
// FREE_ALL does nothing: the heap doesn't know what it gave, so each piece
// goes back with FREE. A size above 0 converts to C's size_t, a u64,
// unchanged. The procedure asks C for exactly what it's given: Basic's
// helpers never ask it for 0 bytes, whose result C leaves to each C
// library, nor give it null to resize or free.
heap_allocator_proc :: (mode: int, size: int, old_size: int, old_memory: *void, data: *void) -> *void {
    if mode == ALLOCATE { return __malloc(cast(u64) size); }
    if mode == RESIZE { return __realloc(old_memory, cast(u64) size); }
    if mode == FREE { __free(old_memory); }
    return null;
}

// The heap as an Allocator. It needs no data.
HEAP_ALLOCATOR :: Allocator.{heap_allocator_proc, null};

// Type information (spec 3.16): Jai's, trimmed to Rig's types.
// type_info(T) (spec 5.19) gives a *Type_Info, the address of a
// description of T laid out as the structs below, and an Any pairs a
// value's address with its type's description. The compiler writes each
// description, once per type, in the program's read-only data (spec 10.3),
// and in the interpreter's memory while compiling (spec 10.8), laid out as
// these structs are: src/types.h mirrors them. A description is read-only:
// writing one is a memory error no check catches (spec 8.6).
//
// Every description starts with a Type_Info, whose tag says its kind. Each
// kind's struct holds that Type_Info as its first field, info (Rig has no
// using), so a *Type_Info whose tag says the kind is cast to the kind's
// struct: cast(*Type_Info_Struct) t when t.tag == TYPE_INFO_STRUCT.

// The kinds, a Type_Info's tag (constants until enums), numbered as Jai's
// tags are.
TYPE_INFO_INTEGER :: 0;
TYPE_INFO_FLOAT :: 1;
TYPE_INFO_BOOL :: 2;
TYPE_INFO_STRING :: 3;
TYPE_INFO_POINTER :: 4;
TYPE_INFO_PROCEDURE :: 5;
TYPE_INFO_VOID :: 6;
TYPE_INFO_STRUCT :: 7;
TYPE_INFO_ARRAY :: 8;

// What every description starts with: its kind, then the type's size and
// alignment in bytes (spec 3.11; void's are 0 and 1). A float, bool,
// string or void (TYPE_INFO_FLOAT, _BOOL, _STRING, _VOID) is described by
// this alone: a float's size says which it is.
Type_Info :: struct { tag: int; size: int; alignment: int; }

// TYPE_INFO_INTEGER: s8 to u64, and int. int and s64 are two types with
// two descriptions, the same in all but their addresses.
Type_Info_Integer :: struct { info: Type_Info; signed: bool; }

// TYPE_INFO_POINTER: *T, and the description of the T it points at
// (void's for a *void).
Type_Info_Pointer :: struct { info: Type_Info; pointer_to: *Type_Info; }

// TYPE_INFO_PROCEDURE: a procedure type, its parameters' types and its
// results', in order, whether it's called as C calls (#c_call), and whether
// its last parameter is a ..T, whose type is then described as []T.
Type_Info_Procedure :: struct {
    info: Type_Info;
    parameter_types: []*Type_Info;
    result_types: []*Type_Info;
    is_c_call: bool;
    is_variadic: bool;
}

// TYPE_INFO_STRUCT: a struct, by its declared name, and its fields in
// order, none for a struct with none. A struct that holds a pointer to
// itself is described by pointing back at its own description: the data is
// cyclic, not infinite.
Type_Info_Struct :: struct { info: Type_Info; name: string; members: []Type_Info_Struct_Member; }

// One field of a struct: its name, its type's description, and where it is
// in the struct, in bytes from its start (spec 3.9).
Type_Info_Struct_Member :: struct { name: string; type: *Type_Info; offset: int; }

// TYPE_INFO_ARRAY: an array, [N]T, or a view, []T, which array_kind tells
// apart (ARRAY_FIXED or ARRAY_VIEW); the description of T; and N, or -1 for
// a view. A string is no view here: it has its own tag, TYPE_INFO_STRING.
Type_Info_Array :: struct { info: Type_Info; element_type: *Type_Info; array_kind: int; count: int; }

// A Type_Info_Array's array_kind.
ARRAY_FIXED :: 0;
ARRAY_VIEW :: 1;

// A value of any type: its type's description and its address. Every value
// converts to an Any implicitly (spec 3.16), a place by its own address, any
// other value by a temporary copy's, which lasts to the end of the
// statement; an Any converts as itself. It is an ordinary struct, so it has
// a description of its own, a struct named Any. The program reads the value
// by casting value_pointer once type says what it is.
Any :: struct { type: *Type_Info; value_pointer: *void; }

// The context (spec 7.10): what code deep in a call chain needs without
// every caller handing it down, Jai's way. Every Rig procedure but main and
// a #c_call one is given the caller's context, a *Context it isn't given in
// the source, and context names it; main starts with default_context.
// push_context runs a block with another (spec 6.13). It holds the
// allocator code takes its memory from when it's handed none, and the
// temporary storage.

// A page of temporary storage, asked of the storage's overflow_allocator
// when the region before it, the first block or the newest page, can't
// hold an allocation: this header, then size bytes. The header is 24
// bytes, a multiple of 8, so the bytes after it are aligned to 8 when the
// page is. start is where its bytes start in the storage's count
// (Temporary_Storage.occupied, which counts from the first block's start),
// so the current page's own offset is occupied - start, found without
// walking the pages.
Temporary_Storage_Page :: struct { previous: *Temporary_Storage_Page; size: int; start: int; }

// Memory that is given out from one end and given back all at once:
// allocations from its first block, data's size bytes, then from pages
// asked of overflow_allocator, the newest first in overflow_pages. occupied
// is how far into the storage's count allocations reach, from the first
// block's start, counting the padding that aligns them and the unused
// tails of regions a page replaced; high_water_mark is the largest it has
// been.
Temporary_Storage :: struct {
    data: *u8;
    size: int;
    occupied: int;
    high_water_mark: int;
    overflow_allocator: Allocator;
    overflow_pages: *Temporary_Storage_Page;  // newest first
}

// The context: the allocator code takes its memory from when it's handed
// none, and the temporary storage.
Context :: struct { allocator: Allocator; temporary_storage: *Temporary_Storage; }

// The bytes of Preload's temporary storage's first block, and of the
// smallest page any storage asks for, its header included
// (temporary_allocator_proc).
TEMPORARY_STORAGE_SIZE :: 32768;

// The context main and each #run start with, and the one a #c_call
// procedure, which C calls with none, pushes for itself when it has no
// other: the heap, and Preload's temporary storage. __runtime_init sets it
// up.
default_context : Context;

// Preload's one temporary storage, which default_context holds, and its
// first block, as u64s so its allocations are 8-aligned.
__temporary_storage : Temporary_Storage;
__temporary_storage_data : [TEMPORARY_STORAGE_SIZE / 8]u64;

// Sets default_context up, before main's first line and each #run's code:
// its allocator the heap, its temporary storage Preload's, whose first
// block is __temporary_storage_data. The pointers are set here, since a
// global's value can't hold another global's address (spec 4.3). Once: a
// second call does nothing. #c_call, so it uses no context itself.
__runtime_init :: () #c_call {
    if default_context.temporary_storage != null { return; }
    __temporary_storage.data = cast(*u8) *__temporary_storage_data[0];
    __temporary_storage.size = TEMPORARY_STORAGE_SIZE;
    __temporary_storage.overflow_allocator = HEAP_ALLOCATOR;
    default_context.allocator = HEAP_ALLOCATOR;
    default_context.temporary_storage = *__temporary_storage;
}

// C's memmove, under Preload's own name, as __malloc is: the temporary
// allocator's RESIZE copies with it.
__memmove :: (destination: *void, source: *void, count: u64) -> *void #foreign libc "memmove";

// The temporary allocator's procedure. It works on the current context's
// temporary storage, context.temporary_storage, so data is unused: a
// push_context with another storage makes it allocate from that one. A
// program's own storage needs data, size and overflow_allocator set, and
// its data and overflow_allocator's memory aligned to 8, as the heap's is,
// for its allocations to be.
//
// It allocates from the current region, the newest page's bytes after its
// header or, with no page, the first block. The region's own offset is
// occupied - start (0 for the first block), since a page starts in the
// storage's count where the region before it ended, rounded up to a
// multiple of 8: so occupied keeps counting through the pages,
// high_water_mark is its largest value, and the newest page's offset is
// found without walking the pages. The rounding keeps a page's offsets as
// aligned as the count's, so the high water mark is at least the size of a
// first block that would have held everything.
//
// - ALLOCATE starts at the first multiple of 8 at or past the offset, so
//   every allocation is aligned to 8, as any Rig type needs (spec 3.11),
//   when the first block and overflow_allocator's memory are. When the
//   region's rest can't hold size bytes, a page is asked of
//   overflow_allocator, TEMPORARY_STORAGE_SIZE bytes or size with the
//   header when that is larger, and the rest of the region before it goes
//   unused but counts as occupied. Its null is passed on, the storage as
//   it was, and a size whose page, with the header, would be past int's
//   largest gives null too, the storage as it was.
// - RESIZE grows or shrinks old_memory in place when it is the last
//   allocation and the region holds size bytes from it. Otherwise it
//   allocates anew and copies the old bytes that fit, or gives null with
//   old_memory as it was.
// - FREE does nothing. The memory goes back with everything else at the
//   next reset.
// - FREE_ALL is a reset: every page goes back to overflow_allocator, newest
//   first, and the first block is handed out again from its start. Memory
//   used after a reset is a memory error no check catches (spec 8.6). The
//   high water mark stays.
temporary_allocator_proc :: (mode: int, size: int, old_size: int, old_memory: *void, data: *void) -> *void {
    LARGEST :: 9223372036854775807; // int's largest value
    storage := context.temporary_storage;
    page := storage.overflow_pages;
    base := storage.data;
    region_size := storage.size;
    start := 0;
    if page != null {
        base = cast(*u8) page + size_of(Temporary_Storage_Page);
        region_size = page.size;
        start = page.start;
    }
    offset := storage.occupied - start;
    if mode == ALLOCATE {
        at := (offset + 7) & ~7;
        // Compared as size against what is left, rather than at + size
        // against the region's size, so that a size near int's largest
        // can't wrap the sum. at may be past the region's end, when offset
        // is within 7 of it, and then nothing is left.
        if size <= region_size - at {
            storage.occupied = start + at + size;
            if storage.occupied > storage.high_water_mark { storage.high_water_mark = storage.occupied; }
            return base + at;
        }
        // A page that big, with its header, has no int size.
        if size > LARGEST - size_of(Temporary_Storage_Page) { return null; }
        bytes := TEMPORARY_STORAGE_SIZE;
        if size_of(Temporary_Storage_Page) + size > bytes { bytes = size_of(Temporary_Storage_Page) + size; }
        overflow := storage.overflow_allocator;
        fresh := cast(*Temporary_Storage_Page) overflow.proc(ALLOCATE, bytes, 0, null, overflow.data);
        if fresh == null { return null; }
        fresh.previous = page;
        fresh.size = bytes - size_of(Temporary_Storage_Page);
        // A region whose size isn't a multiple of 8, a large page's, would
        // otherwise start the page at a count that isn't one. The gap
        // counts as occupied, as the tail does.
        fresh.start = (start + region_size + 7) & ~7;
        storage.overflow_pages = fresh;
        storage.occupied = fresh.start + size;
        if storage.occupied > storage.high_water_mark { storage.high_water_mark = storage.occupied; }
        return cast(*u8) fresh + size_of(Temporary_Storage_Page);
    }
    if mode == RESIZE {
        // The last allocation ends where the region's offset is. old_size
        // within the offset keeps old_memory in the region, and its own
        // offset is then the region's less old_size.
        if old_memory != null && old_size <= offset && cast(*u8) old_memory + old_size == base + offset {
            from := offset - old_size;
            if size <= region_size - from {
                storage.occupied = start + from + size;
                if storage.occupied > storage.high_water_mark { storage.high_water_mark = storage.occupied; }
                return old_memory;
            }
        }
        memory := temporary_allocator_proc(ALLOCATE, size, 0, null, data);
        if memory == null { return null; }
        count := old_size;
        if size < count { count = size; }
        // A count of 0 or less copies nothing, and never reaches C as a
        // size_t that wrapped.
        if count > 0 { __memmove(memory, old_memory, cast(u64) count); }
        return memory;
    }
    if mode == FREE_ALL {
        overflow := storage.overflow_allocator;
        while page != null {
            previous := page.previous;
            overflow.proc(FREE, 0, 0, page, overflow.data);
            page = previous;
        }
        storage.overflow_pages = null;
        storage.occupied = 0;
    }
    return null;
}

// The temporary storage as an Allocator. Its procedure finds the storage
// through the context, so it needs no data.
TEMPORARY_ALLOCATOR :: Allocator.{temporary_allocator_proc, null};
